1. Deployed URL
----------------
Part 1 (PHP): http://webdev.aut.ac.nz/bvf2703/ (uploaded to the webdev server)
Part 2 (React): Deployed locally or via GitHub Pages / Vercel (static build from part2/dist/)


2. Technology Stack
--------------------

Part 1 — Server-side Assignment (assign/)
  Frontend:       HTML5, CSS3, Vanilla JavaScript (ES6+)
  Backend:        PHP 8.x
  Database:       MySQL (on webdev.aut.ac.nz)
  Communication:  Fetch API (asynchronous JSON)

Part 2 — React Application (part2/)
  Frontend:       React 19, Vite 6
  Routing:        Client-side page switching (state-based SPA)
  State:          React Context API (BookingContext, ThemeContext)
  Map:            Leaflet 1.9 + react-leaflet 5.0
  Styling:        CSS custom properties (dark/light theme)
  Backend:        Reuses the same PHP API from Part 1 via Vite dev proxy


3. How to Run and Build the Project Locally
--------------------------------------------

Prerequisites:
  - PHP 8.x with pdo_mysql extension enabled
  - MySQL 8.x running on localhost:3306
  - Node.js 18+ and npm

Step 1 — Set up the database:
  Open a terminal and run:
    mysql -u root -e "SOURCE mysqlcommand.txt"
  This creates the bvf2703 database and the bookings table.

Step 2 — Configure environment:
  Copy .env.example to .env in the project root and fill in your local DB credentials:
    DB_HOST=localhost
    DB_NAME=bvf2703
    DB_USER=root
    DB_PASSWORD=

Step 3 — Start the PHP backend (from the project root):
    php -S localhost:8080

Step 4 — Install React dependencies and start the dev server:
    cd part2
    npm install
    npm run dev

Step 5 — Open the application:
  Open http://localhost:5173 in your browser.
  The Vite dev proxy automatically forwards /api/* requests to the PHP backend at localhost:8080.

Building for Production:
    cd part2
    npm run build
  The optimised static files are generated in part2/dist/.
  These files can be deployed to any static hosting service (GitHub Pages, Vercel, Netlify, etc.).

Deploy to webdev server (Part 1 only):
  1. Connect to webdev.aut.ac.nz via SFTP (Host: webdev.aut.ac.nz, User: bvf2703).
  2. Upload all files from the assign/ folder to htdocs/assign/ on the server.
  3. Run the SQL commands from mysqlcommand.txt on the webdev MySQL server.
  4. Access via: http://webdev.aut.ac.nz/bvf2703/assign/booking.html


4. Microservice API Endpoints
------------------------------

booking.php — POST (creates a new booking)
  Parameters (FormData):
    cname    (required)  Customer name
    phone    (required)  Phone number (10-12 digits)
    snumber  (required)  Street number
    stname   (required)  Street name
    unumber  (optional)  Unit number
    sbname   (optional)  Suburb
    dsbname  (optional)  Destination suburb
    date     (required)  Pickup date (YYYY-MM-DD)
    time     (required)  Pickup time (HH:MM)

  Response (JSON):
    { "success": true, "bookingRef": "BRN00001", "pickupDate": "09/04/2026", "pickupTime": "14:30" }

admin.php — GET endpoints:
  ?action=search                                    Returns unassigned bookings within 2 hours
  ?action=search&bsearch=BRN00001                   Returns exact booking match
  ?action=list                                      Returns all bookings (Part 2)
  ?action=assign&ref=BRN00001&status=assigned        Assigns a taxi to the booking
  ?action=delete&ref=BRN00001                        Deletes a booking (Part 2)

admin.php — POST endpoint:
  action=update, ref=BRN00001, customer_name, phone, etc.   Updates booking fields (Part 2)

Local endpoint:  http://localhost:8080/booking.php, http://localhost:8080/admin.php
Remote endpoint: http://webdev.aut.ac.nz/bvf2703/booking.php, http://webdev.aut.ac.nz/bvf2703/admin.php


5. Feature Descriptions
-------------------------

Part 1 Features:
  1. Booking Form — Customer fills in pickup details; date/time pre-filled with current values.
  2. Client-side Validation — Required fields, phone format (10-12 digits), date/time not in past.
  3. Async Submission — Form data sent via fetch() POST; JSON response displayed without page reload.
  4. Booking Reference — Auto-generated incremental BRN format (BRN00001, BRN00002, ...).
  5. Admin Search — Search by exact reference number, or view unassigned bookings within 2 hours.
  6. Taxi Assignment — One-click assign button; status updates to "assigned" in real time.

Part 2 Additional Features (4 extensions):
  1. Full CRUD — Create, Read, Update, and Delete bookings via the Admin Panel with a
     card-based UI and edit modal. Users can modify any booking field and delete records.
  2. Dashboard with Statistics — Displays total bookings count, assigned vs unassigned counts,
     and a bar chart showing bookings per day for the last 14 days.
  3. Dark/Light Mode — Theme toggle button in the sidebar (desktop) and header (mobile).
     Uses CSS custom properties for instant switching. Theme preference persisted in localStorage.
  4. Interactive Map — Leaflet map centered on Auckland showing pickup and destination locations
     as markers based on suburb name geocoding. Clicking a marker shows booking details.


6. Testing Instructions
------------------------

Example booking references: BRN00001, BRN00002, BRN00003
Sample test data:
  - Customer Name: John Smith
  - Phone: 0211234567
  - Street Number: 42
  - Street Name: Queen Street
  - Suburb: Auckland CBD
  - Destination: Ponsonby
  - Date: (current date)
  - Time: (current time + 30 minutes)

Manual Testing Checklist:
  [ ] Booking form validates required fields (shows error if empty).
  [ ] Phone validation rejects non-numeric and wrong-length inputs.
  [ ] Date/time in the past is rejected by both client and server.
  [ ] Successful booking displays reference number, date, and time.
  [ ] Admin search by reference returns exact match.
  [ ] Empty search returns unassigned bookings within 2 hours.
  [ ] Assign button changes status to "assigned" and disables the button.
  [ ] Part 2: Edit modal updates booking details correctly.
  [ ] Part 2: Delete removes the booking from the list.
  [ ] Part 2: Dashboard statistics update after bookings change.
  [ ] Part 2: Map shows markers for recognized Auckland suburbs.
  [ ] Part 2: Theme toggle switches between dark and light mode.
  [ ] Part 2: Active page and theme persist across page reloads.
  [ ] Responsive layout works on mobile (tab bar) and desktop (sidebar).

Browser Compatibility:
  Tested on: Google Chrome (latest), Microsoft Edge (latest), Firefox (latest).


7. Known Limitations
---------------------

  1. Suburb geocoding is static — The map uses a predefined dictionary of Auckland suburb
     coordinates. Suburbs not in the dictionary will not appear on the map.
  2. Booking reference generation — Uses COUNT(*) + 1 which may produce duplicates if
     bookings are deleted and re-inserted. A production system should use a dedicated sequence.
  3. No authentication — The admin panel has no login or access control. In a real system,
     admin endpoints should be protected with session-based or token-based authentication.
  4. No pagination — The admin booking list loads all records at once. For large datasets,
     server-side pagination would be needed.
  5. PHP credentials hardcoded (Part 1) — Required by the webdev server environment.
     Part 2 uses environment variables via .env for local development.


8. Reflection on AI-Supported Development Process
---------------------------------------------------

This project was developed with the assistance of an AI coding tool (Cursor IDE with Claude).

How AI was used:
  - Scaffolding: Generating the initial project structure, React components, and CSS styling.
  - Debugging: Identifying incorrect SQL column names, PHP extension configuration issues,
    and JavaScript date parsing bugs that caused validation failures.
  - Best practices: Suggesting PDO prepared statements for SQL injection prevention, input
    validation on both client and server sides, and CSS custom properties for theming.
  - Responsiveness: Implementing the mobile tab bar and card-based layout to replace tables
    on small screens.

What I learned from AI assistance:
  - AI is excellent at generating boilerplate code and suggesting design patterns, but the
    developer must understand the code to debug and adapt it to specific requirements.
  - The assignment required very specific HTML attribute names (e.g., name="cname",
    name="bsearch"), which the AI initially missed. Careful review against the specification
    document was essential to ensure compliance.
  - AI-generated code sometimes needs to be restructured to match deployment constraints
    (e.g., hardcoded credentials for the webdev server vs. environment variables for local dev).
  - The iterative process of reviewing AI output, testing, and refining helped me understand
    the full stack (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PHP, MySQL, React) at a deeper level than writing
    everything from scratch would have in the available time.
  - AI tools work best when given clear, specific prompts and when the developer actively
    reviews each piece of generated code rather than accepting it blindly.
